WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.115
Book Three, Proposition Seven.

2
00:00:02.115 --> 00:00:26.290
If on the diameter of a circle a point be taken which is not the center of the circle, and from the point segments fall upon the circle, that will be greatest on which the center is, the remainder of the same diameter will be least, of the rest the nearer to the segment through the center is always greater than the more remote, and only two equal segments will fall from the point on the circle, one on each side of the least.

3
00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:34.650
Let A B C D be a circle with diameter A D and center E, let F be a point on A D other than E, and from F let F B, F C, and F G fall upon the circle.

4
00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:39.473
Join the radii B E, C E, and G E by Postulate One.

5
00:00:39.473 --> 00:00:49.160
Then on E F at the center E, construct the angle F E H equal to angle G E F by One Twenty-Three, and join F H.

6
00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:55.349
In triangle B E F, the two sides E B and E F are greater than B F by One Twenty.

7
00:00:55.349 --> 00:01:02.489
Since radius A E equals E B, the whole segment F A equals E B plus E F, so F A is greater than F B.

8
00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:19.797
Moreover, in triangles B E F and C E F, radii B E and C E are equal, E F is common, and angle B E F is greater than angle C E F; by the Hinge Theorem, One Twenty-Four, F B is greater than F C, and by the same reason F C is greater than F G.

9
00:01:19.797 --> 00:01:25.783
Next, in triangle G E F, G F plus F E is greater than radius E G, which equals E D.

10
00:01:25.783 --> 00:01:31.048
Subtracting E F from each leaves F G greater than F D; thus F D is least.

11
00:01:31.048 --> 00:01:41.577
Because G E equals E H, E F is common, and angle G E F equals angle F E H, triangles G E F and H E F are congruent by One Four, so F G equals F H.

12
00:01:41.577 --> 00:01:53.981
If any third segment F K were also equal to F G, then F K would equal F H—making the nearer to the segment through the center equal to the more remote, which is impossible.

13
00:01:53.981 --> 00:02:06.097
Therefore, F A is greatest, F D is least, nearer segments exceed more remote ones, and only two equal segments fall from F on the circle, one on each side of the least.

14
00:02:06.097 --> 00:02:07.900
As was required to prove.

15
00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:17.125
Proposition Three Seven establishes the strict monotonicity and bilateral symmetry of distance from an interior eccentric point to a circle.

16
00:02:17.125 --> 00:02:27.063
As Heath and De Morgan observe, Euclid reduces circular distance comparison to the triangle inequality of One Twenty and the Hinge Theorem of One Twenty-Four.

17
00:02:27.063 --> 00:02:37.000
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one-hundred-five unites forty-five ancestor nodes from Book One into a single metric law for eccentric points within a circle.

